---
import CarpeItDoc from '../../layouts/CarpeItDoc.astro';
import { foundingMigrationSummary, foundingApplicationsSummary, existingBookingSummary } from '../../data/carpeitPricing';
import { carpeitOperator, complaintsBookUrl } from '../../data/carpeitOperator';

// Coordinate app_config.terms_version with this revision before activating the new pricing model.
const lastUpdated = '17 October 2026';
const email = carpeitOperator.email;
const mail = (subject: string) => `mailto:${email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(subject)}`;
---

<CarpeItDoc
  title="CarpeIt — Terms of Service"
  description="The terms for renting and lending water sports gear on CarpeIt."
  heading="Terms of Service"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    These terms are the agreement between you and CarpeIt when you use the app to rent gear or
    lend it. They also set out what renters and owners agree with each other. Please read them;
    you accept them in the app before you start, and again whenever they change.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Who we are and what CarpeIt is</h2>
  <p>
    CarpeIt is a marketplace operated by {carpeitOperator.name} (NIPC {carpeitOperator.nipc}),
    registered at {carpeitOperator.address} (“CarpeIt”, “we”). You can reach us at
    <a href={mail('CarpeIt')}>{email}</a>. This address is also our single point of contact
    for users and for authorities under the EU Digital Services Act; we answer in English and
    Portuguese.
  </p>
  <p>
    CarpeIt lets riders, schools and shops (“owners”) offer kitesurf, surf, wing foil and SUP
    equipment for rent, and lets other people (“renters”) book it. CarpeIt provides the app,
    takes payments through Stripe, holds deposits under the procedure in section 8, and keeps
    the record of each rental. CarpeIt does not own, inspect or supply the gear, and is not a
    party to the rental itself: the rental is a contract between the renter and the owner.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Private and business owners</h2>
  <p>
    Every listing shows whether the owner is a <strong>private individual</strong> or a
    <strong>business</strong> (a “trader”), as the owner has declared.
  </p>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Renting from a private individual:</strong> EU consumer-protection rules that apply to contracts with businesses do not apply to the rental contract itself. Your rights against the owner are those of general civil law. These terms and the deposit procedure still apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Renting from a business:</strong> consumer law applies to the rental, and you have the rights EU and Portuguese consumer law gives you against a trader.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Owners who rent out gear as part of a trade, business or profession must register as a
    business. Before their listings go live, business owners give CarpeIt their legal name,
    address, phone and trade register number, and certify that they comply with applicable EU
    law. CarpeIt makes reasonable efforts to check this information (Digital Services Act,
    Art. 30).
  </p>

  <h2>3. Your account</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>You must be at least 18 and able to enter into a binding contract. You confirm this in the app before you start.</li>
    <li>Keep your sign-in secure. You are responsible for what happens on your account.</li>
    <li>Use accurate information, and one account per person. A school or shop adds a business profile to a personal account.</li>
    <li>You can delete your account at any time from Profile in the app, once you have no rental in progress. See <a href="/carpeit/delete-account/">Delete your account</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Listing gear</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Only list gear you own or are authorised to rent out, that is safe and in good working order, and that matches its description and photos, including any known defects or repairs.</li>
    <li>You set the daily price, the deposit, and when the gear is available. Keep your availability up to date.</li>
    <li>The pickup address you enter is shown only to renters whose booking you accept. The public listing shows an approximate area.</li>
    <li>CarpeIt may remove listings that break these terms or the law (see section 13).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Seller details and tax reporting</h3>
  <p>
    Before you can lend, you give CarpeIt your seller details: your legal name, date of birth
    (private owners), tax identification number (NIF) and the country that issued it, address
    and phone, and, for businesses, your trade register number. Under Council Directive (EU)
    2021/514 (“DAC7”) and Decree-Law 41/2023, CarpeIt must collect these details, report the
    income owners earn through it to the Portuguese Tax Authority (Autoridade Tributária e
    Aduaneira, AT) once a year where DAC7 applies, and give each owner a copy of what was
    reported. These details are never shown to other users.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Booking</h2>
  <p>
    A renter chooses dates and sends a booking request. Their card is authorised for the rental
    price when they request, but not charged. The owner then has 72 hours to accept or decline.
    If the owner accepts, the rental price is charged and the booking is confirmed. If the owner
    declines, or doesn't answer in time, the authorisation is released and nothing is charged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before paying, the renter confirms that they have the skill and experience for the gear and
    the conditions, that they will check the gear before use, and that they will follow local
    rules. The confirmation is recorded with the booking.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each rental is for specific dates, so the 14-day right of withdrawal for distance contracts
    doesn't apply to it (Decree-Law 24/2014, art. 17(1)(l), which excludes leisure services for
    a specific date or period). You can cancel under section 9 instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="owner-fees">6. Prices, fees and payouts</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Renters pay the rental price shown before they book, which is the total for the rental. CarpeIt doesn't add a fee on top for renters, and the price doesn't depend on how you pay. Any deposit is shown separately (section 8).</li>
    {/* Rates mirror src/data/carpeitPricing.ts and the backend commission_tiers table; change them together, with advance notice. */}
    <li>CarpeIt charges owners a commission, a percentage of the rental price deducted from the rental payment before the owner is paid. Until the new pricing model activates, the current commission remains 8% for every owner. Once it activates, the default rate is 12% (Standard Partners). Founding Partners pay 8% and Volume Partners 10%; these rates are given by CarpeIt's approval, and their eligibility and duration are governed by the published programme terms. Any VAT due on CarpeIt's commission is included in the rate, so it is everything CarpeIt takes from the owner.</li>
    <li>The commission is only charged when a rental goes ahead. If the renter gets a full refund, CarpeIt gives back its commission and the owner pays nothing on that rental. The rate and the amount the owner receives are shown before a listing is published and on every booking.</li>
    <li>{foundingMigrationSummary}</li>
    <li>{foundingApplicationsSummary} Closing applications does not remove existing Founding Partners’ eligibility.</li>
    <li>{existingBookingSummary}</li>
    <li>CarpeIt will tell owners about any change to their commission rate in advance, as the law requires.</li>
    <li>Owners are paid through Stripe. To accept bookings you must set up payouts with Stripe and accept <a href="https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account">Stripe's Connected Account Agreement</a>.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for any taxes due on what you earn.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. How listings are shown, and reviews</h2>
  <p>
    Search shows active listings in the area you choose that are available for your dates and
    match the sport and any filters you set, newest first. Nobody can pay to be shown higher,
    and CarpeIt doesn't rank its own interests above anyone's. Ratings show the average of
    verified reviews: only the renter and the owner of a completed rental can review each
    other, once, within 60 days of the rental ending. CarpeIt doesn't edit reviews, and removes
    them only when they break section 12.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Deposits</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>If a listing has a deposit, a hold for that amount is placed on the renter's card from about 24 hours before pickup, and kept while the gear is out. It is not a charge.</li>
    <li>Card holds expire after a few days, so for longer rentals (more than about 5 days) CarpeIt renews the hold before it expires: a new hold for the same amount is placed and the old one is released.</li>
    <li>The hold is released when the owner confirms the gear came back as expected, or automatically 24 hours after the return if the owner hasn't made a claim.</li>
    <li>If the hold can't be placed or renewed, pickup can't go ahead (or the renter is asked to update their card) until it can.</li>
    <li>The deposit is the most CarpeIt will take from a renter for damage through the app.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>9. Cancellations</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Renters</strong> can cancel a request at any time for free. After the owner accepts, cancelling is free until 48 hours before pickup and the rental price is refunded in full; after that, it isn't refunded.</li>
    <li><strong>Owners</strong> can cancel an accepted booking, and the renter is always refunded in full. Owners who cancel repeatedly may lose access to lending.</li>
    <li>The exact refund is shown in the app before you confirm a cancellation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>10. Pickup, return and the condition record</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>At pickup</strong>, the app asks for photos of the gear, following prompts for that kind of equipment. Either person can take them. The other person must then either agree that they show the gear's condition, or record their own check. Pickup can't be confirmed until one of those has happened. If you disagree with the other person's photos, say what's wrong and take your own before you accept the gear.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos should be taken in the app with the camera.</strong> The app records whether each photo was taken in the app or chosen from the library. Library photos carry less weight, because their date can't be verified.</li>
    <li><strong>Problems found after pickup.</strong> If the renter finds a fault the pickup check missed (for example when rigging), they can report it with photos in the app for 3 hours after pickup. The report becomes part of the rental record.</li>
    <li><strong>At return</strong>, the renter photographs the gear again and marks it as returned. The owner then has 24 hours to confirm the return or make a claim (section 11).</li>
    <li>The renter must return the gear on time, clean, and in the condition it was received in, apart from normal wear and tear.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>11. Damage claims and disputes</h2>
  <p>
    If gear comes back damaged, with parts missing, or isn't returned, the owner can open a claim
    in the app before confirming the return, stating what happened and how much of the deposit
    they ask for, with photos unless the gear wasn't returned. The renter then has 48 hours to
    accept or dispute it; nothing is charged in the meantime. If the renter accepts, that amount
    is taken from the deposit hold and paid to the owner, and the rest is released.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the renter disputes the claim or doesn't answer in time, the hold stays in place while a
    CarpeIt reviewer looks at the rental record: pickup and return photos and whether both
    people agreed to them, pickup problem reports, messages, and anything either person sends
    in within the time we give. Normal wear and tear and faults recorded at pickup are not
    charged to the renter, and amounts are based on reasonable repair cost or the fair value of
    the item at the time of the rental, not its replacement cost when new. CarpeIt then decides
    how much of the deposit, if any, goes to the owner, never more than the owner asked for,
    releases the rest, and tells you both why.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Appeal.</strong> Either of you can ask for the decision to be reviewed by replying
    within 14 days with new information. The review is done by someone not involved in the
    first decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    CarpeIt's decision settles only what happens to the deposit; it doesn't decide who is
    legally liable. Damage beyond the deposit, injury and any other claim are between the renter
    and the owner, and either of you keeps every right to take them to a Julgado de Paz, an
    arbitration centre or the courts (section 18). On request, CarpeIt will share the rental
    record with both of you and with the court.
  </p>

  <h2>12. How to behave</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Be honest, respectful and on time. No harassment, hate, threats, spam or scams.</li>
    <li>Keep bookings and payments in the app. Arranging payment outside CarpeIt removes the protections of the deposit and the rental record.</li>
    <li>Reviews must describe a real rental, honestly.</li>
    <li>You can report a user, message or listing from the app, and block anyone you don't want to hear from. Once blocked, neither of you can message or book the other.</li>
    <li>CarpeIt reviews reports and may remove content or suspend accounts that break these rules (section 13).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>13. Reporting illegal content, moderation and appeals</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Reporting.</strong> Anyone, even without an account, can report a listing, message, review or user they believe is illegal or breaks these terms: in the app, or by email to <a href={mail('CarpeIt report')}>{email}</a>. Explain why, and include a link to or description of the content. We confirm receipt and tell you what we decided.</li>
    <li><strong>Our decisions.</strong> We may remove or restrict content, suspend lending, or suspend or close an account. These decisions are taken by a person, not by automated systems. We tell the person affected what we decided, the facts and the rule or law it is based on, and how to contest it (a statement of reasons), before or when the decision takes effect, unless the law or a safety risk prevents it.</li>
    <li><strong>Appeals and redress.</strong> You can contest any moderation decision within 6 months by replying to that message or emailing <a href={mail('CarpeIt appeal')}>{email}</a>. Someone not involved in the original decision reviews it. You can also use a certified out-of-court dispute settlement body under Art. 21 of the Digital Services Act, or go to court.</li>
    <li>Reports made in bad faith, or repeated unfounded reports, may lead us to stop processing your reports for a time, after a warning.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>14. Safety and your responsibility</h2>
  <p>
    Water sports are dangerous and can cause serious injury. Renters are responsible for judging
    whether gear, conditions and their own ability are suitable, for checking the gear before
    use, and for using it within their skill level and local rules. Owners are responsible for
    the safety and condition of what they lend, and for disclosing known defects. CarpeIt
    doesn't check gear, people or spots, and doesn't provide insurance. We recommend that owners
    insure valuable gear for rental use, and that renters have personal accident and liability
    cover.
  </p>

  <h2>15. Liability</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>CarpeIt is responsible for providing the app and the payment and deposit services described here with reasonable care and skill. CarpeIt is not responsible for the gear itself, for the rental contract between renter and owner, for what renters and owners do, or for loss caused by events outside our reasonable control.</li>
    <li>Nothing in these terms excludes or limits liability for death or personal injury caused by our negligence, for fraud, wilful misconduct or gross negligence (art. 809 of the Portuguese Civil Code and Decree-Law 446/85), or any other liability that cannot be excluded or limited by law. Nothing in these terms affects your statutory rights as a consumer.</li>
    <li>Subject to the point above: if you lend on CarpeIt <strong>as a business</strong>, CarpeIt's total liability to you is limited to the fees CarpeIt received from you in the 12 months before the claim. If you are a <strong>consumer</strong>, CarpeIt is liable for loss that was a foreseeable result of our breach of these terms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>16. Ending your use</h2>
  <p>
    You can stop using CarpeIt and delete your account whenever you like, once you have no
    rental in progress. CarpeIt may suspend or close accounts that break these terms or the law,
    as described in section 13.
  </p>
  <p>
    For schools and shops lending as a business, CarpeIt gives at least 30 days' notice, with
    reasons, before ending their access for good, except for a serious or repeated breach of
    these terms, a legal obligation, or a safety risk (Regulation (EU) 2019/1150, art. 4).
    Business owners can raise any complaint about CarpeIt by email, and CarpeIt answers within
    15 days.
  </p>

  <h2>17. Changes and law</h2>
  <p>
    If these terms change, the new version is published here and announced in the app at least
    15 days before it applies, unless a law or a safety issue requires a change sooner. For
    business users, this notice is always at least 15 days, as Regulation (EU) 2019/1150
    requires. You'll be asked to accept the new version in the app; if you don't want to, you
    can close your account before it applies. These terms are governed by Portuguese law. If
    you are a consumer, you also keep the protection of the mandatory laws of the country where
    you live, and can bring a claim there.
  </p>

  <h2>18. Complaints and dispute resolution</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Contact us first</strong> at <a href={mail('CarpeIt complaint')}>{email}</a>. We reply within 15 working days.</li>
    <li><strong>Complaints book.</strong> You can also complain in CarpeIt's <a href={complaintsBookUrl}>electronic complaints book (Livro de Reclamações Eletrónico)</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Alternative dispute resolution.</strong> If you're a consumer and we can't resolve a dispute, you can take it to a consumer arbitration centre. Under Lei 144/2015, CarpeIt refers you to <a href="https://www.cniacc.pt">CNIACC – Centro Nacional de Informação e Arbitragem de Conflitos de Consumo</a> (www.cniacc.pt), or the consumer arbitration centre for your area. The full list of approved entities is on the <a href="https://www.consumidor.gov.pt">Portal do Consumidor</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>19. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about these terms? Email <a href={mail('CarpeIt terms')}>{email}</a>
    or write to {carpeitOperator.name}, {carpeitOperator.address}.
    For how your data is handled, see the <a href="/carpeit/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>
</CarpeItDoc>
